00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:09.880
and her kids left, and I went back,

00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:12.880
sort of like I was on Cloud Nine, as you can imagine.

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:15.880
And I went back to the crew, the camera crew,

00:15:15.880 --> 00:15:19.880
Dickie and Martin and John, the director.

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:24.880
I said, God, wasn't that absolutely fantastic?

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:29.880
And John said, um, but you didn't say anything about the thumb.

00:15:29.880 --> 00:15:31.880
LAUGHTER

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:35.880
I said, well, I know we had all this marvellous material.

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:39.880
He said, well, I... we didn't really shoot any.

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:41.880
I said, what do you mean?

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:44.880
He said, well, I didn't want to run out of film, you know,

00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:47.880
until you were going to go on about the thumb and forefinger.

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:50.880
And it was only after I'd been there for about ten minutes

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:52.880
that Martin, the cameraman, said,

00:15:52.880 --> 00:15:55.880
look, why don't we take just a few shots of what's going on?

00:15:55.880 --> 00:15:58.880
You know, it'll make the boys in the country laugh.

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:01.880
They didn't get it at all. Forgive me, John.

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:05.880
Does that remain for you the moment,

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:10.880
the kind of justify the entire business of observing animals?

00:16:10.880 --> 00:16:13.880
Oh, it was... I mean, it's simply in a class by itself.

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:16.880
I mean, it was just on a... it's a different experience.

00:16:16.880 --> 00:16:19.880
I mean, I've never experienced anything like it,

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:21.880
and I will never experience it again.

00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:24.880
And the reason is that gorillas...

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:28.880
well, gorillas and chimpanzees are so like...

00:16:28.880 --> 00:16:31.880
Well, I said all that in the film, but they are like us,

00:16:31.880 --> 00:16:34.880
and you actually really felt that you were communicating something.

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:37.880
Now, you can't communicate with an earwig, you know?

00:16:37.880 --> 00:16:39.880
And you can't... You really can't...

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:42.880
I don't think you can communicate with...

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:44.880
I don't know, well, with anything much, really.

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:47.880
I mean, people will communicate with dogs, yes,

00:16:47.880 --> 00:16:50.880
but somehow the whole business of being a kind of primate,

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:52.880
you understand between the two of them.

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:54.880
And so that was just miraculous,

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:57.880
and it's just an unforgettable moment in my life,

00:16:57.880 --> 00:16:59.880
which I will never forget.

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:01.880
I'm not being allowed to forget it because...

00:17:01.880 --> 00:17:04.880
No, not should you be, because anybody who's ever watched television...

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:07.880
What is the everlasting appeal, do you think, of...

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:09.880
with the public I'm talking about,

00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:11.880
of the kind of programme that you do?

00:17:11.880 --> 00:17:14.880
Because, see, they're not just watched by a few people,

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:16.880
they're watched by a lot of people.

00:17:16.880 --> 00:17:20.880
Well, they're a huge... they're a marvellous thing to be able to turn on on your television.

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:23.880
Road accidents and wars and politicians

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:29.880
and advertisements and nonsenses and odiousness.

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:34.880
Suddenly you see something which is amazing,

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:39.880
very often extremely beautiful, unpredictable, full of drama,

00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:41.880
and above all, it's true.

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:44.880
I mean, it's... You know, I mean, no-one's codding anybody.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:46.880
This is actually reality.

00:17:46.880 --> 00:17:48.880
There's nobody making it up, nobody varnishing it.

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:50.880
This is what goes on.

00:17:50.880 --> 00:17:52.880
These other things have life.

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:54.880
The one thing that we share with them,

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:57.880
this mysterious thing that nobody can identify,

00:17:57.880 --> 00:18:00.880
we simply don't know what it means, we don't know where it comes from,

00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:02.880
but they have it and you have it.

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:05.880
So by understanding them better, then,

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:08.880
you're beginning to understand perhaps the great mysteries

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:11.880
about why we're here, who we are, what are we doing?

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:14.880
I don't know if I knew the answers.

00:18:14.880 --> 00:18:16.880
I'm not saying...

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:19.880
Well, what you do, at least what I do,

00:18:19.880 --> 00:18:22.880
is I don't look at animals because I think,

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:25.880
oh, yes, I should be wiser about myself if I look at animals.

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:27.880
I don't believe that.

00:18:27.880 --> 00:18:32.880
What is fascinating about animals is to see how a centipede,

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:38.880
a spider, a frog, a bat, a whale, and you

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:40.880
are all faced with the same problems.

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:44.880
How to find a mate, how to find food, how to raise the kids.

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:47.880
I mean, we all have those problems.

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:50.880
And what is marvellous about natural history is what you see

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:53.880
is that there is an infinite number of different ways

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:55.880
of solving those problems.

00:18:55.880 --> 00:18:57.880
That's what makes natural history fascinating to me.

00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:00.880
The other thing that intrigues me about you, too,

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:06.880
is that you're a scientist and you apply a scientific mind

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:10.880
to observing the natural world that we live in.

00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:13.880
But at the same time, I think you're a romantic as well.

00:19:13.880 --> 00:19:16.880
Is there a paradox in the two?

00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:18.880
Can a scientist be romantic?

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:20.880
Oh, absolutely.

00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:21.880
Of course.

00:19:21.880 --> 00:19:23.880
I mean, that's why people are scientists,

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:28.880
because they are enthralled by the way the brain works.

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:32.880
Fascinated to know how it is that the world came to be the shape

00:19:32.880 --> 00:19:35.880
that it is and animals work in the way that they work

00:19:35.880 --> 00:19:37.880
and that we work in the way that we work.

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:40.880
And that is romantic. It's extraordinarily romantic.

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:45.880
I mean, the most romantic thing I remember as a child, age seven,

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:47.880
hitting a rock, seeing it fall open

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:52.880
and finding a perfectly preserved shell, beautiful, glistening, shiny,

00:19:52.880 --> 00:19:55.880
of a kind that you've never seen before in your life.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:59.880
And the chap who's with you says,

00:19:59.880 --> 00:20:04.880
that shell is 150 million years old

00:20:04.880 --> 00:20:09.880
and your eyes were the first ever to fall upon it.

00:20:09.880 --> 00:20:12.880
Now, that's...that's romantic, isn't it?

00:20:12.880 --> 00:20:14.880
Certainly is. Of course it is. It's extraordinary.

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:15.880
Great.

00:20:15.880 --> 00:20:16.880
Great.

00:20:16.880 --> 00:20:18.880
I love that. I'm hooked on this stuff, you see.

00:20:18.880 --> 00:20:19.880
Are you?

00:20:19.880 --> 00:20:23.880
If I had my way, that's all I would watch on television.

00:20:23.880 --> 00:20:24.880
I absolutely love it.

00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:27.880
But you did your share of going out, didn't you?

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:29.880
You did the same thing as Sir David,

00:20:29.880 --> 00:20:32.880
but you went out and you lived in the Arctic Circle for a while.

00:20:32.880 --> 00:20:35.880
I did. I lived there for ten days and nights.

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:37.880
The film crew would film me in the morning

00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:40.880
and then they'd bugger off about 12 o'clock

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:42.880
and leave me on my own.

00:20:42.880 --> 00:20:44.880
And there was an SAS guy,

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:48.880
he was down at the sea watching for polar bears

00:20:48.880 --> 00:20:51.880
coming...they come in that direction.

00:20:51.880 --> 00:20:53.880
And there were some mountains behind me

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:56.880
and I thought, well, what about albino grizzly bears?

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:58.880
You know?

00:20:58.880 --> 00:21:01.880
But they gave me a gun

00:21:01.880 --> 00:21:03.880
and I would never shoot a polar bear.

00:21:03.880 --> 00:21:05.880
And he said, well, if you...

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:08.880
If you aim right in the middle of the polar bear

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:09.880
and if it's going to attack you,

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:12.880
just move slightly right and shoot one past his head

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:15.880
and he'll get the fright of his life and stop.

00:21:15.880 --> 00:21:16.880
And I believed him.

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:18.880
I never had the opportunity to try.

00:21:18.880 --> 00:21:21.880
But I loved it being completely...

00:21:21.880 --> 00:21:23.880
I was like the only man on the planet.

00:21:23.880 --> 00:21:25.880
I would go for walks, you know,

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:27.880
and I was the only man on Earth.

00:21:27.880 --> 00:21:29.880
It was the best time of my life, total silence.

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:31.880
And you took all your clothes off. I saw that.

00:21:31.880 --> 00:21:34.880
I did. I took my clothes off. I always take my clothes off.

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:37.880
Do you? In every film, I do take my clothes off.

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:38.880
Well, I don't.

00:21:38.880 --> 00:21:41.880
And I look like Porridge with hair on it, you know?

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:46.880
I don't like Porridge with that hair on it.

00:21:47.880 --> 00:21:50.880
We have... No, do I!

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:53.880
We have, actually... Some people have sugar.

00:21:57.880 --> 00:22:00.880
If I can break up this meeting, actually...

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:02.880
I was going to show you, actually,

00:22:02.880 --> 00:22:05.880
the spot I did with you, actually, about your nudity,

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:07.880
your propensity for nudity.

00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:09.880
Now, this is a shot from... Oh!

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:11.880
Hello, Dave!

00:22:11.880 --> 00:22:12.880
There he is!

00:22:12.880 --> 00:22:14.880
What a beautiful creature!

00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:17.880
That's a shot from Mrs Brown, isn't it?

00:22:17.880 --> 00:22:21.880
There's something about nudity that's undeniable, you know?

00:22:21.880 --> 00:22:24.880
It's just funny. Everybody's got a funny bum.

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:26.880
Even these bronzed Adonis guys,

00:22:26.880 --> 00:22:28.880
when they run, the bum's a funny thing.

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:31.880
And it just takes all... It's lovely.

00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:34.880
And now I insist on it, and dancing.

00:22:35.880 --> 00:22:38.880
You know, you know that silly dancing when you're naked.

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:40.880
It's such a freeing thing.

00:22:40.880 --> 00:22:42.880
There's a new development?

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:44.880
No, it's over the past few years.

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:46.880
Oh, and Jeremy Iams told me,

00:22:46.880 --> 00:22:49.880
you know, I did it in Orkney around those standing stones.

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:51.880
There's nothing else to do.

00:22:52.880 --> 00:22:54.880
Well, not in Orkney.

00:22:54.880 --> 00:22:58.880
But with the standing stones, I mean, what are you supposed to do?

00:22:58.880 --> 00:23:00.880
You know, and there's a notice that says,

00:23:00.880 --> 00:23:02.880
it is thought that perhaps...

00:23:02.880 --> 00:23:05.880
Well, that means you don't know what it is, do you?

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:07.880
And I think you should throw that notice away.

00:23:07.880 --> 00:23:10.880
And I just put, we don't know what this is.

00:23:10.880 --> 00:23:12.880
Try and leave it the way you found it.

00:23:13.880 --> 00:23:15.880
And I thought, well, let's dance round it.

00:23:15.880 --> 00:23:17.880
I mean, it must have been for something.

00:23:17.880 --> 00:23:19.880
It was probably for dancing round.

00:23:19.880 --> 00:23:21.880
So I danced round it naked.

00:23:21.880 --> 00:23:23.880
But Jeremy Iams was filming there, and I met him,

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:25.880
we had a holiday together in Scotland,

00:23:25.880 --> 00:23:28.880
and he said, you'll never... The locals are doing it now.

00:23:28.880 --> 00:23:30.880
They go up with the wee video camera,

00:23:30.880 --> 00:23:33.880
they go, right, there's no traffic, go for it, John.

00:23:35.880 --> 00:23:37.880
So probably...

00:23:42.880 --> 00:23:46.880
So probably in 20 or 30 years or 50 years,

00:23:46.880 --> 00:23:48.880
the guys who follow you around,

00:23:48.880 --> 00:23:50.880
they'll be up there with a camera saying,

00:23:50.880 --> 00:23:52.880
this is a quaint custom,

00:23:52.880 --> 00:23:54.880
because back thousands of years,

00:23:55.880 --> 00:23:57.880
they're dancing round the stones.

00:23:57.880 --> 00:23:59.880
Nobody knows why.

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:03.880
Are you worried, yes, the two of you,

00:24:03.880 --> 00:24:06.880
that in this phrase now, dumbing down,

00:24:06.880 --> 00:24:09.880
there's been... Yeah...in television,

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:12.880
that the kind of programme that you do will suffer?

00:24:14.880 --> 00:24:16.880
Well, I hope not, and believe not.

00:24:16.880 --> 00:24:20.880
I certainly don't think that the BBC will dumb it down.

00:24:20.880 --> 00:24:24.880
I mean, if public service broadcasting doesn't do that,

00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:27.880
what's the point of having public service broadcasting?

00:24:27.880 --> 00:24:30.880
But public service broadcasting has changed since you got the BBC.

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:33.880
Yes, it has, and there are a lot of other competitors,

00:24:33.880 --> 00:24:36.880
and fine, I mean, that's our job,

00:24:36.880 --> 00:24:39.880
is to deal with the competitors coming up from satellites

00:24:39.880 --> 00:24:41.880
and through cables and so on.

00:24:41.880 --> 00:24:45.880
So a public service broadcasting organisation like the BBC

00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:48.880
has to offer something that no other network can offer.

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:51.880
And doing serious things

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:54.880
and doing some undumbed-down content

00:24:54.880 --> 00:24:56.880
is the one thing it can do.

00:24:56.880 --> 00:24:58.880
And when it gives that up,

00:24:58.880 --> 00:25:03.880
then I would think that it hasn't got much reason to continue to exist.

00:25:03.880 --> 00:25:06.880
Absolutely. I think the BBC is outstanding.

00:25:06.880 --> 00:25:11.880
And all the fights to make it have adverts and cheapen it and all that,

00:25:11.880 --> 00:25:13.880
I would fight it all away.

00:25:13.880 --> 00:25:16.880
And the other thing, apart from educating and entertaining people

00:25:16.880 --> 00:25:18.880
that everybody thinks the media is for,

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:21.880
the motto here is,

00:25:21.880 --> 00:25:24.880
a nation shall speak peace unto nation.

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:27.880
And I think that's a great, great theme to go with.

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:31.880
I mean, our game show is speaking peace unto other nations.

00:25:31.880 --> 00:25:33.880
You know what I mean?

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:36.880
They're throwing green goo over famous people on children's television.

00:25:36.880 --> 00:25:39.880
I think we should fight to keep the...

00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:42.880
There's a programme on public service broadcasting in America,

00:25:42.880 --> 00:25:45.880
and it's every Wednesday, it's a big debate, and it's great.

00:25:45.880 --> 00:25:47.880
It might be abortion or whatever,

00:25:47.880 --> 00:25:50.880
but they all have a huge fight, great debate.

00:25:50.880 --> 00:25:52.880
But at the beginning of the programme,

00:25:52.880 --> 00:25:54.880
the head of the station comes on and says,

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:58.880
the whole point of this programme is to make the cut and thrust of debate

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:02.880
so severe that the only escape is by thinking.

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:05.880
LAUGHTER

00:26:05.880 --> 00:26:09.880
And I thought, yes! Yes, we are not alone!

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:11.880
You know?

00:26:11.880 --> 00:26:13.880
I don't think you should be teaching

00:26:13.880 --> 00:26:16.880
and wagging your index finger at people all the time,

00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:18.880
but you should teach...

00:26:18.880 --> 00:26:21.880
To teach and entertain at the same time is such an extraordinary thing to do.

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:24.880
That's what Sir David's done, wag his finger at all.

00:26:24.880 --> 00:26:27.880
We wouldn't have tolerated him for 40 years wagging his finger at people.

00:26:27.880 --> 00:26:31.880
But, I mean, onward and onward, you've got this new series.

00:26:31.880 --> 00:26:34.880
Well, as long as I can keep vertical and keep moving,

00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:36.880
people push me in front of things, yes.

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:39.880
Yeah, because it's the best job in the world, isn't it, really?

00:26:39.880 --> 00:26:43.880
Well, you make it that. Sir David Attenborough, thank you very much indeed.

00:26:43.880 --> 00:26:45.880
APPLAUSE

00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:47.880
Thank you.

00:26:57.880 --> 00:27:00.880
My thanks to Billy Connolly and to David Attenborough.

00:27:00.880 --> 00:27:03.880
Next week, my guests are Gareth Lineker and Patricia Routledge.

00:27:03.880 --> 00:27:06.880
Until then, from all of us here, a very good night. Good night.

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:15.880
APPLAUSE
